WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.379
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.
We are now going to move to Item 5 which is

00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:17.039
on Primary Care Co-Commissioning. We have
a paper from Ian and I invite you, Ian, please

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:18.560
to introduce it for us.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.560
Ian Dodge: The programme is on track in line
with the paper that the Board considered in

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:36.730
October. This programme of delegating primary
care commissioning functions to CCGs is an

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:43.820
essential part of the new deal for primary
care set out in the Forward View. It is also

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:51.200
a necessary precursor to developing new integrated
models of provision. We need more joined up

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:58.320
commissioning in order to unlock more integrated
provision and it is a vote of confidence in

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.190
Clinical Commissioning Groups.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:07.190
I would like to highlight the joint working
that NHS England has undertaken with CCG leaders

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:15.329
and particularly thank NHS Clinical Commissioners
for their help and their constructive challenge

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.120
and request that the Board authorise the formal
delegation.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.150
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ian. Before I put
that question to the Board, let me invite

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:28.060
some comments and queries to Ian. David?

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.600
David Roberts: Just to say that the Commissioning
Committee has looked at this as well and is

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.390
content with where we are.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.170
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Moira?

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.590
Moira Gibbs: Can I ask Ian on a scale from
nought to ten how confident is he about the

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:49.310
management of the conflicts of interest? Not
from the point of view as aware of you and

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.710
someone who understands it but from the test
of the person on the Clapham omnibus.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:01.710
Ian Dodge: We are confident that we are putting
in place robust arrangements based on the

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.400
new statutory guidance that was agreed by
the Audit &amp; Risk Committee. We have put in

00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:18.190
place training arrangements for lay members
and we have a set of rules which includes

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:25.190
publishing the decisions of the CCG committees,
the open invitation to those committees, for

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:36.140
local Healthwatch members and local authority
Health &amp; Wellbeing boards. At this stage we

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:42.010
are confident that the arrangements we have
devised will be fit for purpose. Clearly,

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:48.720
we will need to continue to pay a keen and
close interest to the practical operation

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.150
of these arrangements once they go live.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.750
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Ed?

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:58.760
Ed Smith: I am just going to add what Ian
said. We have been through this in the Audit

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:05.760
committee. We have also had two sessions with
70% of the CCG Audit Chairs in February. I

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:13.770
cannot say it has been easy to focus minds
on this but I think all the mechanisms that

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:20.930
need to be in place, barring actual working
of it in the system, are now in place. The

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:27.930
proof will be when the Chairs of Audit committees
have to give the assurance at the CCG level

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:35.590
of compliance with the guidance and the requirements.
That is always a test but I think we have

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.519
to recognise that we are in that type of system
where we are not directly responsible for

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:47.930
that. There has been a lot of encouraging
support from the lay members of CCGs about

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:52.150
their role in that. Notwithstanding, I think
it is fair to say that they themselves are

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.890
under pressure of time and volume of work
that they have to do.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.489
Malcolm Grant: Barbara?

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:02.560
Barbara Hakin: I think also it is important
to remember the reach of NHS England in our

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.819
local teams and what a close relationship
many of our local teams have with a small

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:13.819
number of CCGs. Whilst this process has been
managed within the National Framework, the

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:18.030
key work has been done on the ground where
our local teams have actually worked with

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:24.389
every individual CCG going through their application,
understanding the workings within the CCG

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:31.080
and only giving us the confidence at national
level to support the CCGs that we are requesting

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:37.780
now to go forward to delegation. It is because
they have confidence that it is a CCG where

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.720
they do have the proper governance arrangements,
as the local team has seen from the assurance

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.600
oversight and that they can be confident that
conflicts of interest have been managed properly.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.919
I know many conversations have happened at
regional or local level about this, but at

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:59.070
national levels we have had specific conversations
about a couple of CCGs whereby we had to ask

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.750
the local team to delve more deeply into an
issue of conflict of interest before we made

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.330
the final decision to recommend this list
to you.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:11.860
Malcolm Grant: I think those assurances are
absolutely essential. The critical issue I

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.830
think is communication here and communication
not only through our local operational team

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.130
but also nationally. I think the meetings
that Ed has been chairing with Audit chairs

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:28.699
has been opening up a whole array of issues
which we were able to provide training, education,

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:34.770
but actually fundamentally support. I think
it is the transmission of messages about our

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:41.210
expectations of how conflicts of interest
might be identified and prevented and any

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:47.880
damage minimised or mitigated because if we
get that right only then can CCGs command

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.540
the confidence of the communities with which
they work.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:58.540
Moira, thank you for raising that. Are there
other questions to Ian on this paper? If not,

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:07.080
may I ask you then to endorse the proposal
in paragraph 8, which is to approve the delegation

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:13.300
agreements and to authorise the Chief Financial
Officer to execute the new arrangements? Are

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:20.050
we content to do that? Thank you very much.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.350
The next item on the agenda, Item 6, is the
New Care Models programme, an overview of

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.169
this. Ian, back to you.

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:32.860
Ian Dodge: Thank you, Chair. It is with great
pleasure that I introduce this item. There

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:39.180
has been fantastic interest in the NHS in
the New Care Model programme. As Simon said

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.229
in his introductory comments, we have had
hundreds of applicants. Right across the country

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:50.830
people are engaging with different partners
to think about how they re-design care for

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:57.830
their local patients and citizens, I think
going beyond their current institutional boundaries.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.530
What has been really exciting and interesting
is the extent to which this has meant hospitals

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:10.319
talking to GP practices and with social care
with the voluntary sector and with patient

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:11.630
groups.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:18.630
Following the three days of selection at the
shortlisting process at the Oval, the New

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.539
Care Models programme board, which has now
been fully constituted and has met twice and

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:31.229
which I co-chair with Adrian Masters of Monitor.
This is a joint endeavour across the ALBs

00:07:31.229 --> 00:07:38.229
and with other national partners we agreed
29 vanguard sites and areas to develop the

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:45.150
integrated health and care systems of the
future. I think our principles here are very

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:51.419
much about how we support those local areas
and solve their problems and I will come back

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:52.370
to that.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:59.370
I would particularly like to emphasise though
what a difficult set of decisions the Board

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:07.590
had to make, both at the shortlisting stage
and then the final decisions. Inevitably,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:15.460
such selection processes can never be perfect
and there will be many of the applicants who

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:22.460
can be and will be just as successful and
move just as rapidly as the vanguard sites.

00:08:23.949 --> 00:08:30.949
NHS England, Monitor and our partners very
strongly encourages all those interested in

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:39.919
redesigning care, whether it is to form a
multi-specialty community provider, a vertically-integrated

00:08:40.979 --> 00:08:47.040
PACS model, develop better links and care
in care homes or indeed many of the other

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:54.040
types of new care models outlined in the Forward
View, to press on with the same pace that

00:08:54.709 --> 00:08:57.709
they were intending anyway.

00:08:57.709 --> 00:09:04.709
As a little example of that, I had the pleasure
of spending last Friday down on the Kent coast

00:09:06.829 --> 00:09:13.829
in South Kent Coastal CCG talking to local
leaders and patients. I have every confidence

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:23.029
that the application put in by that CCG to
become a MCP can proceed just as quickly as

00:09:26.259 --> 00:09:32.980
the neighbouring successful application in
Whitstable and Canterbury from the Whitstable

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:39.980
Medical Practice. Indeed, South Kent Coastal
CCG is intending to do exactly that.

00:09:40.759 --> 00:09:47.759
I would also like to emphasise the fact that
the vanguard will not just be improving care

00:09:51.179 --> 00:09:58.179
for their local populations. Their significance
is far greater than that. Their collective

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:07.860
voice will be immensely powerful. It will
be immensely powerful in identifying the practical

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:19.319
ambitions and the changes they want to make
in terms of involving patients, involving

00:10:19.319 --> 00:10:26.319
communities in really redesigning care delivery
systems, working through new workforce roles

00:10:26.639 --> 00:10:32.389
and skill mix, as well as some of the more
technical changes that many of them are requesting,

00:10:32.389 --> 00:10:39.149
whether that is to the way in which we regulate
collectively, the way in which contracts work

00:10:39.149 --> 00:10:42.829
and payment mechanisms.

00:10:42.829 --> 00:10:47.459
We in NHS England are determined, with our
national partners, including Monitor and the

00:10:47.459 --> 00:10:54.459
TDA and others, to back the vanguards and
to listen to what they are saying they need.

00:10:57.499 --> 00:11:04.360
We are currently going through an in-depth
process of visiting each of the sites over

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:11.360
a two-day period to identify their aspirations
and their needs and then co-design a national

00:11:12.749 --> 00:11:18.279
support programme where it will be the vanguards
themselves saying to the national partners,

00:11:18.279 --> 00:11:24.329
'We would like you please to solve these problems.'
It will then be our responsibility to respond

00:11:24.329 --> 00:11:31.329
to that with pace and effectively to solve
those local problems. That is our task ahead.

00:11:32.179 --> 00:11:38.540
We are setting ourselves up to be able to
deliver that and I am very confident that

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:41.540
we will succeed in that endeavour.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:46.329
Malcolm Grant: Perhaps we might draw you out
a bit further on it. What do you anticipate

00:11:46.329 --> 00:11:51.119
will be the problems that they will be asking
us to solve for them?

00:11:51.119 --> 00:11:58.119
Ian Dodge: A number of the vanguards are saying
that they would like some permission to develop

00:12:00.109 --> 00:12:07.109
new workforce roles that perhaps blend the
role of a geriatrician and a GP or that look

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:14.069
differently at the way in which out of hospital
nursing operates. They would like support

00:12:14.069 --> 00:12:21.069
from the Royal Colleges to be able to develop
new job descriptions. They would like help

00:12:22.339 --> 00:12:28.799
in understanding the potential of digital
technology to redesign the way in which people

00:12:28.799 --> 00:12:35.799
access care. They would like help in navigating
public procurement arrangements so that they

00:12:36.639 --> 00:12:44.079
can develop these New Models very quickly.
They would like help in understanding contractual

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:52.460
arrangements. We have said in our criteria
that we would expect both MCPs and PACSs to

00:12:52.559 --> 00:13:00.439
be able to hold a larger capitated contract
for a wider range of services. They want to

00:13:00.499 --> 00:13:07.449
understand what the simplest way of blending
commissioner responsibilities are and then

00:13:07.449 --> 00:13:14.449
creating new contracts. Our intention is to
design some new model contracts for the sites

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:23.730
as well as then new payment mechanisms, to
actually design the capitated sums for those

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.899
sites so that they are able to then go live.

00:13:26.899 --> 00:13:33.899
Some of these problems that they are articulating
are common across the different sites. Rather

00:13:34.109 --> 00:13:41.109
than each of them seeking to secure separate
support and develop very similar but marginally

00:13:41.359 --> 00:13:46.919
different solutions, it will probably make
more sense for them to come together as a

00:13:46.919 --> 00:13:52.999
collective and commission single solutions
which they can then choose to use. Indeed

00:13:52.999 --> 00:13:59.290
those solutions can be made available to the
rest of the country at the same time that

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:02.549
the vanguards then are using them.

00:14:02.549 --> 00:14:08.319
I hope that we will then be in a position
where, in a year's time, when we look back

00:14:08.319 --> 00:14:15.269
on what the vanguards will have achieved in
their first year we will not merely get fantastic

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:22.269
examples of 29 unique, completely contextually-specific
solutions that are very difficult for the

00:14:22.269 --> 00:14:28.249
rest of the country to learn from. We will
actually be able to point to a number of places

00:14:28.249 --> 00:14:30.529
that are choosing to do things in a common
way.

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:35.410
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ian. Let me invite
discussion from the Board

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:42.410
Moira Gibb: Thanks, Malcolm. Ian, I was struck
yesterday by how many times people at the

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:49.249
UK Citizen event talked about problems with
communication and how much we needed to up

00:14:49.249 --> 00:14:55.519
our game in relation to communication. You
talked quite a lot about public participation

00:14:55.519 --> 00:15:01.589
in the design of the vanguards, which is the
best way of communication. However, not everyone

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:06.619
can be involved. Is your assumption that,
in a sense, that each one will be doing its

00:15:06.619 --> 00:15:12.259
own communication? Or is there again national
support for that communication and engagement?

00:15:12.259 --> 00:15:18.869
Will they all be different or will there again
be the opportunities of learning what works

00:15:18.869 --> 00:15:19.540
best?

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:25.980
Ian Dodge: I think we have to have a communication
approach that is both local and national at

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:32.980
the same time. How we support local sites
to make sure that they are engaging effectively

00:15:33.329 --> 00:15:39.709
with their local communities. I think that
is very powerful, particularly if they have

00:15:39.709 --> 00:15:45.619
common problems. The extent to which it is
then the vanguard sites and their local populations

00:15:45.619 --> 00:15:52.619
demanding changes or help from the national
bodies, is far more likely to be successful.

00:15:53.059 --> 00:16:00.059
Then secondly, in terms of national communications:
I am building within Samantha Jones's team

00:16:01.549 --> 00:16:08.519
dedicated communication support working closely
with Tim and Tim's team. We are putting resources

00:16:08.519 --> 00:16:15.519
in to making sure that we can provide a very
high quality service. That is our ambition

00:16:15.609 --> 00:16:20.970
for the vanguards and to communicate, not
just the work programme, but the stories of

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:26.079
what is happening in each of these sites.
Not only so that they can learn from each

00:16:26.079 --> 00:16:29.449
other but so that the rest of the country
can see what is going on.

00:16:29.449 --> 00:16:30.259
Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:16:30.259 --> 00:16:36.889
Victor Adebowale: I am just curious. It leads
on from the last question really. How are

00:16:36.889 --> 00:16:43.889
you ensuring that groups outside the NHS system
are engaged in the co-design of some of these

00:16:44.879 --> 00:16:51.879
services? How are you ensuring that not-for-profit
groups etc.? How is that happening?

00:16:52.569 --> 00:16:59.049
Ian Dodge: Firstly, I would say the Forward
View itself. We absolutely placed as core

00:16:59.049 --> 00:17:05.270
design principles in chapter 2 of the Forward
View that when we are designing the New Care

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:10.919
Models, articulated in chapter 3, they had
to be based on the principles of prevention

00:17:10.919 --> 00:17:17.079
and community engagement and citizen empowerment.
Those themes are themes that we continually

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:22.709
emphasise whenever we are discussing New Care
Models with the NHS.

00:17:22.709 --> 00:17:29.709
Secondly, we have then, in establishing the
criteria and designing the approach for expressions

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:37.130
of interest, built in a very clear expectation
that the people applying will be partnering

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:44.309
with other organisation and showing how they
will be engaging patients and their local

00:17:44.309 --> 00:17:51.090
citizens. It is a core design principle and
it is only through the in-depth work that

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:57.220
we are now doing with the sites that we can
understand the scale of that and, also, what

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:02.389
the ambitions are from the vanguard sites
for further support in this area.

00:18:02.389 --> 00:18:10.529
I can absolutely assure you, Victor, that
this will be a core theme right at the heart

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.120
of how the vanguards operate.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:21.880
Tim Kelsey: I think unpacking that conversation
is fundamental to the replicability of the

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:27.779
vanguards, which if we do not achieve that,
then we have just achieved very little, I

00:18:27.779 --> 00:18:32.169
think. One of the things that we were talking
about a bit earlier is the importance of supporting

00:18:32.169 --> 00:18:36.809
particularly the vanguards, but the NHS as
a whole to learn something from other industries

00:18:36.809 --> 00:18:43.549
about what you might loosely call market research,
i.e. the person as an important point of data

00:18:43.549 --> 00:18:49.220
in understanding whether or not something
has had a useful, positive impact on them.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:54.899
Apart from engagement perhaps more conventionally
understood, deep immersion, deliberative,

00:18:54.899 --> 00:18:59.769
co-production with local communities and patients,
we also need to be very, very specific about

00:18:59.769 --> 00:19:05.220
building and supporting vanguards to build
a kind of market research capability or an

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:11.110
insight capability which will enable replication.
I think that has been a gap in a way in the

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:17.039
story of how the NHS and health and care in
general has approached innovation. I think

00:19:17.039 --> 00:19:21.269
that is something which we have been working
on but it hopefully will be a new and embedded

00:19:21.269 --> 00:19:26.419
feature in the way that the vanguards operate
and does involve us talking to some very,

00:19:26.419 --> 00:19:31.519
very different people across the community
at large who we are perhaps not used to speaking

00:19:31.519 --> 00:19:32.970
to.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:39.970
Margaret Casely-Hayford: I think that one
of the things that is going to be really important

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:50.380
here is that when we look at learning that
we can see something that is very simple because

00:19:51.529 --> 00:19:56.870
the essence of all of this is that we are
creating bespoke packages where necessary.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:20:03.870
That essentially means that there are lots
of variations and we could have an incredibly

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:12.230
sort of complex choice for those coming afterwards.
We need to make sure that, not only are the

00:20:13.659 --> 00:20:19.570
learnings properly recorded, but there is
a simplicity in the message. Also, that there

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:26.570
is not a feeling of blame with anything having
been gone wrong. What went wrong? Co-creation

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:32.629
is an enormous amount of opportunity for things
to go awry and we need to be very positive

00:20:32.629 --> 00:20:39.629
about allowing an element of error to be corrected
on route and the learning to be properly recorded

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:50.700
from that to give confidence to go forward.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:57.269
Ian Dodge: I think Margaret you raise two
terribly important points here. One of which

00:20:57.269 --> 00:21:04.269
is our appetite for the vanguard to innovate
and to try different things. That is their

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:12.230
whole point. Now, clearly, whatever they do
we need to make sure that services are safe

00:21:14.559 --> 00:21:21.559
and effective but not all of the new approaches
will necessarily deliver the quality improvements

00:21:24.309 --> 00:21:31.309
and the efficiency gains that they are intending.
That is inevitable if people are innovating.

00:21:33.059 --> 00:21:39.570
In a way it will be a sign of success that
we have that innovation occur.

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:46.570
The second point you pick up in your comment
there is around the need for excellent evaluation

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:52.559
and for that evaluation not to be something
which is separate and happens after the event

00:21:52.559 --> 00:21:59.559
but is an integral part of the design and
the implementation process. We are building

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:07.919
a new Operational Research &amp; Evaluation function
within NHS England, rather than something

00:22:07.919 --> 00:22:14.330
we are outsourcing. I have appointed a Head
of that unit, Charles Tallack, and he will

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:21.330
be establishing a team to be able to provide
real time learning and evaluation and develop

00:22:21.759 --> 00:22:28.740
that methodology with sites. This will comprise
both a set of common standard metrics and

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:32.529
those metrics we will co-design with the local
sites.

00:22:32.529 --> 00:22:39.529
However, not just a set of outcome metrics
because if we were simply saying the only

00:22:39.639 --> 00:22:43.970
thing that matters is the outcome metrics
we would not be having a structured New Care

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:50.970
Model programme. We are placing a bet here
that there are certain ways of redesigning

00:22:52.769 --> 00:22:58.629
care that are likely to be more successful.
That is something that the NHS has responded

00:22:58.629 --> 00:23:05.120
to very positively. That means we also need
to get inside the black box of 'What is a

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:11.080
successful multi-specialty community provider
or vertically-integrated system?' and so on

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:17.669
and understand the logic model of what is
going on so that we can understand why these

00:23:17.669 --> 00:23:21.740
models are successful and why they may not
be.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:28.230
John Burn: Ian, it is very exciting to see
these lifting off and I have the sense of

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:32.340
watching rockets lift off. My hope is that
these are rockets that are about to put things

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:37.769
into orbit that will then become a reference
for the rest of the NHS planet. My concern

00:23:37.769 --> 00:23:42.580
is that they are actually rockets that are
prefaced to the New Year's Eve fireworks display

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:46.909
and they make a big bang and then they all
fall back to earth again, as charred remains

00:23:46.909 --> 00:23:53.909
and everyone says, 'Wasn't that fun?' Forgive
the levity but you know what I am saying.

00:23:54.259 --> 00:23:58.100
Reading your last sentence it is a beautifully
crafted sentence that says, 'We build communities

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:01.840
to support, share good practice, accelerate
the skill of change more broadly, whilst not

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:05.409
diluting the focus and thus the impact for
patients.' Which is a bit like reading my

00:24:05.409 --> 00:24:12.409
horoscope. It is a carefully worded phrase
that does not quite commit us to saying we

00:24:12.539 --> 00:24:19.129
are going to deploy this more widely than
the community of support. My question is in

00:24:19.129 --> 00:24:26.129
addition to that early 360 degree re-evaluation
and looking towards replicability in communities

00:24:28.419 --> 00:24:33.470
of support, if I was looking at this in a
scientific way, what I would really like to

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:38.029
do is randomly distribute the ones that worked
to places that were not expecting them and

00:24:38.029 --> 00:24:42.889
say, okay, we would like now to try this in
a new place, not full of enthusiasts who are

00:24:42.889 --> 00:24:49.519
going to want it to win, and say will it work
in the real NHS where it has not got those

00:24:49.519 --> 00:24:54.929
unrecognised but very critical leaders that
make it work in certain places?

00:24:54.929 --> 00:25:01.070
Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, John, for
your comment. On your first point as to whether

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:08.770
or not this is placing things in orbit or
merely New Year's Eve, I very much expect

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:15.090
the Board to hold the Executive to account
for the fact that we achieve the former rather

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:28.730
than the latter. I think the challenge you
raise about wider spread is one that we are

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:36.620
deeply mindful of. What is the best way in
which we achieve that? I think there are some

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.749
learnings here from other programmes about
what has been most successful and what has

00:25:40.749 --> 00:25:46.350
not. We will be working specifically with
the Kings Fund and a number of other national

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:53.350
partners to try and make sure that this is
not just about those 29 sites.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:26:02.070
I would emphasise that for the changes to
work, for the vanguard to be successful, it

00:26:06.779 --> 00:26:13.779
means engaging deeply and effectively within
those sites with individual GPs, with patients,

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:23.509
with nurses and it requires people to choose
to change how they are currently delivering

00:26:23.509 --> 00:26:30.509
care. That comes as a result of deep partnership
working and significant organisational development.

00:26:34.179 --> 00:26:41.179
That takes time and it requires people wanting
to change. The one caution I have is against

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:49.480
saying that becoming an MCP or a PACS is the
only solution and saying that this is now

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:54.789
what we are expecting the whole of the NHS
to do or indeed to suggest that we would be

00:26:54.789 --> 00:27:01.789
mandating that. It would then cut across the
energy that is necessary for change.

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:04.620
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:10.249
Ciaran Devane: Think about the Integration
Pioneers, which was another portfolio of very

00:27:10.249 --> 00:27:14.419
similar things and what have we learned from
that experience and what went well and what

00:27:14.419 --> 00:27:21.120
did not? Sometimes there was the support felt
like you maybe would have all just gone on

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:27.629
and done it without the support. Have we captured
that? Have we captured what has worked? Have

00:27:27.629 --> 00:27:32.940
we shared that? Have we wrapped up the cons
around it because again that was all part

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:35.519
of what that programme was intended to achieve?

00:27:35.519 --> 00:27:39.820
Ian Dodge: Thank you, Ciaran, for raising
that. The Integration Pioneers are doing a

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:46.820
very important work. There are now 25 of them.
We announced a second wave earlier in the

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:54.240
year. Indeed, South Kent Coastal CCG and the
MCP proposals in part were born out of the

00:27:57.129 --> 00:28:04.129
Integration Pioneer work in Kent. We have
had a very successful one-year-on event with

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:11.450
all of the Integration Pioneers over the last
couple of months and published a report on

00:28:11.450 --> 00:28:18.450
the achievements of the individual Pioneers.
There are some fantastic local examples, some

00:28:19.039 --> 00:28:26.039
of which are now well-known nationally. For
example, the work that Kernow CCG has undertaken

00:28:26.090 --> 00:28:33.090
with Age UK in relation to delivering services
differently for frail, elderly patients, which

00:28:33.929 --> 00:28:40.929
has not only been delivering improved outcomes
and reduced emergency utilisation but it has

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:46.830
also, interestingly, I think the figures are
something like one in either 40 or 25 of the

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:53.830
people who received the service are now volunteering
with Age UK to be able to deliver the service

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:59.809
that they themselves received in a really
fantastic example of sustainability. There

00:28:59.809 --> 00:29:03.070
are many fantastic specific examples.

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:09.970
What I think we could do better with the Integration
Pioneers is to think about what are those

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:15.200
common elements and indeed it was our learning
from the Integration Pioneers that has informed

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:22.200
the design of the New Care Models programme.
Managerially, the Integration Pioneers will

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:30.580
be overseen as part of a single integrated
portfolio by Samantha Jones. We now have an

00:29:31.929 --> 00:29:38.929
effective national local Pioneer support group
which has met on a number of occasions, which

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:44.889
is co-chaired with Frances Martin from Worcestershire
Integration Pioneers.

00:29:44.889 --> 00:29:51.269
We have been prioritising particular issues
that the Pioneers care about most, of which

00:29:51.269 --> 00:29:57.740
I would highlight the challenges of navigating
through information governance as one. There

00:29:57.740 --> 00:30:04.740
was a National Information Governance summit
jointly arranged with the Pioneers, the Department

00:30:05.529 --> 00:30:11.590
of Health and the many other agencies. We
had about 50 people in the room on Monday

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:17.999
to try and work out what the best solutions
are for that. That work is a test of our ability

00:30:17.999 --> 00:30:21.690
to be able to solve problems for local communities.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:28.690
Victor Adebowale: A question had formed in
my mind. The whole point of doing this is

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:41.360
it is supposed to change business-as-usual
at some point. I am just curious about how

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:48.360
it is affecting the rest? How is it affecting
the rest of the system? I guess it is connected

00:30:48.639 --> 00:30:55.590
in some ways to John Burn's thoughts which
are if it is going to be sustainable then

00:30:55.590 --> 00:31:02.590
what you cannot have is this programme doing
excellent work but actually a project is not

00:31:03.749 --> 00:31:10.279
impacting on the rapid shift of business-as-usual.
This at some point needs to become what we

00:31:10.279 --> 00:31:17.279
do around here. I am just wondering about
that and the risk of it becoming almost in

00:31:18.879 --> 00:31:25.499
competition. We have two systems. We have
the business-as-usual and then we have this.

00:31:25.499 --> 00:31:29.860
This actually becomes a bit of a pain because
of the pressures of day-to-day. I am not so

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.200
sure it is fair to ask you that question but
it springs to mind.

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:39.200
Ian Dodge: I think it is a fantastic question
and one we need to be very mindful of. I would

00:31:41.230 --> 00:31:48.230
say that the vanguards are not just a set
of site-specific pieces of work. They are

00:31:48.549 --> 00:31:55.549
not merely pilots. I would say that for two
reasons. One of which is, in a way we will

00:31:57.220 --> 00:32:04.220
be redesigning national rules and policy arrangements
through what the vanguards want. They signify

00:32:07.489 --> 00:32:13.950
something greater than merely what is happening
in that local site. Then when that work is

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:20.950
successful - and I say when rather than if
- it will enable other areas to slipstream

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:27.090
and take advantage of the changes that the
vanguard will help bring about.

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:33.320
I think, secondly, I would say that they are
part of a wider movement. In a way the Forward

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:40.320
View was not generated de novo. These ideas,
many of them have been within the NHS and

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:47.899
the health and care system for some time.
I was struck, Victor, by the fact that there

00:32:47.899 --> 00:32:54.659
are 170 proposals and expressions of interest
and there are many more who did not express

00:32:54.659 --> 00:33:00.440
interest in becoming multi-specialty community
providers. That I believe is unthinkable a

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:07.440
year ago, certainly two years ago. There is
a different mood afoot in many, many parts

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:14.440
of the country and an appetite, particularly
in general practice to rethink the model of

00:33:15.889 --> 00:33:22.889
care delivery. I am heartened that we will
avoid your two worlds, twin track risk.

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:31.600
Victor Adebowale: I just wondered when, as
the Board, we would expect to see signs of

00:33:32.909 --> 00:33:38.289
life in other areas of our business that this
was infecting in some ways? I am just curious

00:33:38.289 --> 00:33:42.399
as to how we are going to do that? It is just
a thought.

00:33:42.399 --> 00:33:49.399
Margaret Casely-Hayford: I was going to ask
whether it would be helpful to the Executive

00:33:51.419 --> 00:33:58.179
for the Board to have it in mind that we take
stock of the progress of the vanguards and

00:33:58.179 --> 00:34:05.179
at some point have a policy push, policy support
for the movement of the vanguard forward.

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:14.830
As Ian quite rightly says, there is a difference
between being pilots and a vanguard. I assume

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:21.710
you choose your words carefully, as you always
do and vanguard is expecting something to

00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:26.770
follow in the wake, in the slipstream, to
use your words. That is something that I think

00:34:26.770 --> 00:34:32.010
the Board needs to get behind. Otherwise,
Victor's fears will be realised and you will

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:39.010
get the continuing parallel systems. If so,
at what point would you like us to do so?

00:34:40.339 --> 00:34:46.679
Ian Dodge: That is a very helpful suggestion
and we will follow that up. I think there

00:34:46.679 --> 00:34:53.679
is a practical question around the band width
of the national organisations to provide intensive

00:34:56.629 --> 00:35:02.829
support equally to all parts of the country.
One of the reasons why we have only selected

00:35:02.829 --> 00:35:09.829
29, and that is quite a lot of sites, is that
is at the limit of our ability to do intensive

00:35:10.369 --> 00:35:17.369
work. There is, I think, a profound design
question for us as to how we can then not

00:35:19.799 --> 00:35:26.799
merely give permission to the rest of the
country - and make it easier for them to adopt

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:33.599
new care models, slipstreaming in the wake
or alongside, almost as an unofficial vanguard,

00:35:33.599 --> 00:35:39.980
alongside the official vanguard - there is
also a very big question around what is the

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:45.450
scale of transformational funding that may
be required. We need to understand what the

00:35:45.450 --> 00:35:51.490
double running costs are so that we can calculate
what that would look like nationally. We need

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:58.490
to understand also what the wider support
would be needed regionally in order to achieve

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:10.410
the wider spread. As well as how we can help
build quality improvement capabilities within

00:36:11.119 --> 00:36:12.359
individual institutions.

00:36:12.359 --> 00:36:18.780
David Roberts: I think, Margaret, you raised
an important point and I was going to cover

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:24.290
this in the oral report later. We thought
about this extensively at the Commissioning

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:29.079
Committee yesterday. One of the things that
we have asked is that we effectively have

00:36:29.079 --> 00:36:35.559
a list of the top ten barriers and that each
month we knock off a barrier. Therefore there

00:36:35.559 --> 00:36:40.829
is a mechanism by which we both support the
Executive and also hold them to account. The

00:36:40.829 --> 00:36:46.799
fundamental issue here is how do we take the
learnings that we receive from the 29 vanguards

00:36:46.799 --> 00:36:52.500
and indeed that we will get from the others
who, as Ian says, will be pressing ahead outside

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:58.339
of the formal support mechanism? Rather than
have everyone thinking about these things

00:36:58.339 --> 00:37:02.190
there are some of these things that nationally
we can solve. There are a lot of them that

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:06.200
are absolutely local and we should leave the
people best able to make those decisions,

00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:10.819
i.e. the local people, to take those. There
will be some that we really need to crack

00:37:10.819 --> 00:37:15.869
on and focus. They will inevitably be the
thorny difficult ones that we probably have

00:37:15.869 --> 00:37:20.990
tried to look at in the past and not done
a good job of and will need to build a lot

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:25.650
of momentum with our partner bodies. It is
unlikely they will be purely within our remit

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:31.980
and they will range from technology to workforce
to incentives to contracting to all of those

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:38.980
things. We need to find a practical way, even
to some extent, of an interim solution whilst

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:44.619
we think about the proper longer term robust
policy solution. That will be the trade-off.

00:37:44.619 --> 00:37:51.369
However, speed, aggressively attacking the
problem and finding solution will be the order

00:37:51.369 --> 00:37:52.839
of the day.

00:37:52.839 --> 00:37:57.040
Malcolm Grant: I see Ian nodding in enthusiastic
assent.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.089
David Roberts: Good, because he is doing the
work.

00:37:59.089 --> 00:38:03.690
Malcolm Grant: That is exactly it. I think
we have had a really good discussion of this

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:10.690
but let us be clear that this is an exercise
that carries very high risks. I think its

00:38:11.619 --> 00:38:17.150
greatest risk is also its greatest strength.
What we have done is actually invert the normal

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:24.150
model of reform within the NHS. The traditional
model is one of central prescription, top-down,

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:34.470
structural change and equality across the
nation. Our biggest risk is that in inverting

00:38:34.619 --> 00:38:41.619
that there is a different set of responsibilities
fall on our shoulders. First of all, I think

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:46.319
the model that you have adopted of running
a competition and having it peer reviewed

00:38:46.319 --> 00:38:53.210
by others in the same competition was really
quite innovative and outstanding. Again, a

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:54.359
departure from the norm.

00:38:54.359 --> 00:38:59.859
I think, secondly, the model in which the
successful vanguards then commission us to

00:38:59.859 --> 00:39:05.220
do stuff is an inversion of the normal model
and again highly innovative. Innovation carries

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:09.940
risks and I think the biggest risks are those
which have been identified by colleagues around

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:14.849
the table, which actually go to our ability
to satisfy the expectations that we have created

00:39:14.849 --> 00:39:20.109
amongst the vanguard sites.

00:39:20.109 --> 00:39:27.109
There have been so many metaphors this morning.
I prefer the slipstream rather than the exploding

00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:37.140
sky rockets metaphor. Actually it imposes
upon us a long-term requirement to be true

00:39:39.790 --> 00:39:45.270
to what we have committed to. Just put this
into the context. It was only last October

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:49.440
that we came forward with the Five Year Forward
View. We announced what we were doing. It

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:56.230
is now March. We have made significant progress.
What we are going to want to hold you to account

00:39:56.230 --> 00:40:03.230
for, Ian, I think is a regular update on progress,
metrics that we can understand what is happening

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:11.190
with the vanguards. Also to the extent we
can assess it. What is the impact elsewhere?

00:40:11.799 --> 00:40:17.369
What is the impact on those that are not vanguards
but are nonetheless coming in behind the mood

00:40:17.369 --> 00:40:24.369
of change? Given that this is not something
that is being determined by us or by government

00:40:25.539 --> 00:40:30.980
but is very much attuned to the enthusiasm
of clinicians and others who want to improve

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:34.510
the quality of the offer to patients in their
area.

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:39.099
I think the final issue and Victor, I think,
has stressed it very well, is let nobody be

00:40:39.099 --> 00:40:44.190
left behind. There is an issue and this is
one of the great risks. If you are not going

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:48.880
to prescribe something top-down, if it is
determined by the pace of change and enthusiasm

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:55.880
in local areas, you do get a differentiated
effect. We have always to worry that this

00:40:55.900 --> 00:41:02.890
should not increase the gap of health inequalities
rather that it should have the effect of reducing

00:41:02.890 --> 00:41:09.890
it. Those are simply a few of the criteria
by which we would seek to assess our progress

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:11.039
with the project.

00:41:11.039 --> 00:41:18.039
Ian Dodge: May I, share, just very briefly,
one last comment? The national uniform approach

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:26.450
has not solved the problems that we face in
terms of health inequalities. In order to

00:41:26.619 --> 00:41:33.619
fix health inequalities we need to take a
more differentiated approach in local communities.

00:41:33.819 --> 00:41:39.049
I profoundly believe that actually taking
the vanguard approach and actually many of

00:41:39.049 --> 00:41:45.530
the sites are in deprived communities where
the health gap is greatest, is more likely,

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:49.440
as a strategy, to work than having a single
uniform approach.

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:54.470
Malcolm Grant: I think we completely agree.
I do not think anyone around the table believes

00:41:54.470 --> 00:42:01.470
that top-down uniform approaches are the answer
to any of the problems that we have presently

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:02.670
got.

00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:09.670
Good. Thank you very much and please will
you convey to your team our expression of

00:42:10.569 --> 00:42:15.900
admiration for what has been achieved and
our expectation that that will be continued?

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:16.309
Good. Thanks a lot

